### \(\phi\) Meson Reconstruction

#### 3.4.1 Reconstruction Cuts

The cuts used to reconstruct \(\phi\) meson signals are listed in Table 3.8. The decay mode we made use of is \(\phi\to K^{+}K^{-}(BR=(49.2\pm 0.5)\%,\tau=(1.55\pm 0.01)\times 10^{-22}s)\) thus topological cuts that we exploited in \(\Omega^{-}(\bar{\Omega}^{+})\) study are not applicable, due to its shorter lifetime. The contamination of electrons in kaon samples may introduce significant mis-identified \(\phi\) from \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs. Because the major source of \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs is photon conversion and the dip-angle of the conversion is usually small, it is effective to apply a cut on that quantity to largely remove the contamination. The dip-angle is shown in Figure 3.21 and can be computed as follows:

\[\delta=\cos^{-1}(\frac{p_{T,1}p_{T,2}+p_{z,1}p_{z,2}}{p_{1}p_{2}}) \tag{3.13}\]

A significant level of background is still expected in the invariant mass distribution of \(\phi\) because of the lack of effective topological cuts. The strategy we take to reconstruct the background shape is called event-mixing, or mixed-events.

