#### 5.1.2 Antibaryon-Baryon ratio

Figure 5.5 depicts the collision energy dependence of anti-baryon over baryon ratios for \(\Omega,\Xi\), and \(\Lambda\)s. The data shows a very clear hierarchy of the ratios (\(\bar{\Omega}/\Omega>\bar{\Xi}/\Xi>\bar{\Lambda}/\Lambda\)) and the three trends are separated further as the energy decreases. This result is consistent with predictions made by statistical thermal models [1][2][3][4][5].

It has been proposed in Ref. [31] that a statistical model (with parameters of particle mass, degeneracy factor, baryon/strangeness/charge chemical potentials (\(\mu_{B},\mu_{S},\mu_{Q}\)), a strangeness saturation factor (\(\gamma_{s}\)) and a chemical freeze-out temperature \(T\)) can be used to describe the anti-baryon and baryon yields. If we take the log of the anti-baryon to baryon ratio, many unknown parameters will be canceled out and the expression below emerges:

\[\ln(\bar{B}/B)=-2\mu_{B}/T+\mu_{S}/T\cdot\Delta S \tag{5.1}\]

where \(\Delta S\) is the strangeness difference between measured baryon and anti-baryon (e.g., \(\Delta S=2\) for \(\Lambda(\bar{\Lambda})\), \(\Delta S=6\) for \(\Omega(\bar{\Omega})\)). Given the measured \(\bar{B}/B\) ratio and \(\Delta S\), a linear relationship between \(\mu_{B}/T\) and \(\mu_{S}/T\) can be obtained. Figure 5.7 shows the fitting using data points measured in Au+Au at 14.5 GeV. If we take \(\mu_{B}/T\) and \(\mu_{S}/T\) as the independent

